    "measurementTechnique": [
      "BEFORE THE FLIGHT\nSelect an applicable drone and register the drone.\n**Verify on the websites of DroneDeploy and Pix4D that the most recent version is compatible with your drone.\nUse https://www.faa.gov/uas/getting_started/register_drone to register drone.\n**Affix registration stickers to the drone and carry a physical registration card during flights.\nDo a demo flight and process data through the preferred software to get familiar with operating the drone and processing data.\nPLAN THE DRONE FLIGHT\nI decided to use DroneDeploy for this mission, so I used DroneDeploy to plan the flights as well.\n**Drone Flight Instruction (Incl. contacts, cautions, tips).pdf is under SETx_UIFL > Theme 2 Co-Design > Visualization under Deep Uncertainty.\nCertain flights may require permissions from LAANC (Low Altitude Authorization and Notification Capability); To acquire permissions 72 hours prior to departure because LAANC will not send any permission within 72 hours.\nTo get well-prepared flight paths because flight height can affect resolution and to improve the quality of 3D structures with a perimeter or crosshatch flight path.\nFLIGHT PERMISSIONS\nPermissions were obtained from local authorities for each flight.\nDURING THE DRONE FLIGHT\nFrequently check Battery level and SD card storage when fly autonomously.\nSet up timed(2s) shooting for getting a more stable drone camera gimbal to get higher quality photos.\nFrequently perform data QA/AC for each location.\nStore data to the DroneDeploy or any backup storage for each location.\nAFTER THE DRONE FLIGHT\nCheck the quality of the photos, then delete mistaken and low-resolution photos.\nUpload aerial photos to SETx_UIFL > Theme 2 Co-Design.\nUpload aerial photos to DroneDeploy to generate point cloud and 3D model.\nCheck accuracy of the map and 3D model\nCheck Orthomosaic Resolution and any issue."
    ],
